🔥 Architecture Explanation
1. VPC

👉 The VPC is the base network for the project
It provides isolation and allows you to control IP ranges, subnets, and routing

In your project:

One custom VPC is created
Public and private subnets are created across multiple Availability Zones
This improves organization and availability

2. Public Subnets

👉 Public subnets are used for internet-facing components

In your architecture:

The NLB is placed in public subnets
The NAT Gateway is also placed in a public subnet

Why?

Because these resources need internet connectivity

3. Private Subnets

👉 Private subnets are used for internal workloads

In your architecture:

EKS worker nodes are deployed in private subnets

Why?

Because application compute should not be directly exposed to the internet
This is a common production design pattern

4. Internet Gateway

👉 The Internet Gateway allows public subnet resources to communicate with the internet

Used for:

NLB traffic
Public subnet outbound access

5. NAT Gateway

👉 The NAT Gateway allows resources in private subnets to access the internet for outbound traffic only

Example:

EKS worker nodes may need internet access to:

Pull container images
Install updates
Access AWS APIs

But they still remain private because they do not receive direct inbound traffic from the internet

6. Network Load Balancer

👉 The NLB acts as the network entry point

In your project:

Terraform creates:

NLB
Target group
Listener

Why NLB?

Because NLB works at Layer 4 and is commonly used for TCP-based workloads and Kubernetes integrations

7. Route53

👉 Route53 gives a friendly DNS name for users

In your project:

A Route53 record points to the NLB DNS name

Why?

Instead of using raw AWS load balancer DNS, users can access the platform using a proper domain name

8. EKS Cluster

👉 EKS is the managed Kubernetes control plane

In your project:

Terraform creates:

EKS cluster
IAM role for cluster

This gives you a managed Kubernetes environment on AWS

9. EKS Managed Node Group

👉 Worker nodes run the actual application pods

In your project:

Terraform creates:

Node IAM role
Required IAM policy attachments
Managed node group in private subnets

Why managed node group?

AWS manages node provisioning and lifecycle more easily than self-managed worker nodes

🔥 Module-Wise Explanation
1. Root Module

👉 The root module is the entry point of the project

Files:

main.tf
variables.tf
outputs.tf
backend.tf
versions.tf

Responsibility:

Configure provider
Call child modules
Pass variables
Expose outputs

2. VPC Module

👉 This module handles all networking

It creates:

VPC
Public subnets
Private subnets
Internet Gateway
Public route table
Private route table
Elastic IP
NAT Gateway
Route table associations

Why separate module?

Because networking is reusable and should be isolated from application logic

3. NLB Module

👉 This module handles external traffic entry

It creates:

Network Load Balancer
Target group
Listener

Why separate module?

Because load balancing is its own responsibility and can be reused in other projects

4. Route53 Module

👉 This module handles DNS mapping

It creates:

Route53 record

Why separate module?

Because DNS is a separate concern from networking and compute

5. EKS Module

👉 This module handles Kubernetes platform creation

It creates:

IAM roles
IAM policy attachments
EKS cluster
Managed node group

Why separate module?

Because EKS setup is more complex and should be reusable and easier to maintain

🔥 Step-by-Step Project Flow
1. Configure Terraform Version and Provider

👉 First, define:

Terraform version
AWS provider version
AWS region

Why?

This ensures consistent execution across systems

2. Configure Remote Backend

👉 Store Terraform state remotely in S3 and use DynamoDB for locking

Why?

To support team collaboration
To avoid local state issues
To prevent concurrent `apply` conflicts

3. Define Root Variables

👉 Create variables for:

region
environment
project_name
zone_id
route53_record_name
vpc_cidr
public_subnets
private_subnets
azs
cluster_name
node_group_name
node size values
common tags

Why?

To avoid hardcoding and make the project reusable across environments

4. Create VPC Module

👉 Build the full network foundation

Terraform creates:

Custom VPC
3 public subnets
3 private subnets
Internet Gateway
NAT Gateway
Routing between public and private layers

Why first?

Because all other modules depend on network resources

5. Output VPC Resources

👉 The VPC module outputs:

VPC ID
Public subnet IDs
Private subnet IDs

Why?

These outputs are needed by the NLB and EKS modules

6. Create NLB Module

👉 Use the VPC ID and public subnet IDs to create:

NLB
Target group
Listener

Why?

This provides the external traffic endpoint

7. Create Route53 Module

👉 Use the NLB DNS output to create a DNS record

Flow:

NLB DNS -> Route53 record

Why?

This makes the platform accessible by domain name

8. Create EKS Module

👉 Use public and private subnet outputs from the VPC module

Terraform creates:

Cluster IAM role
EKS cluster
Node IAM role
Required managed policies
Managed node group

Why private subnets for nodes?

For better security and cleaner production architecture

9. Expose Important Outputs

👉 Root outputs should display:

NLB DNS
VPC ID
Public subnet IDs
Private subnet IDs
EKS cluster name
EKS endpoint

Why?

These are useful for verification, integration, and documentation

10. Run Terraform Commands

👉 Typical execution flow:

terraform init
terraform fmt
terraform validate
terraform plan
terraform apply

Why this order?

`init` prepares providers and backend
`fmt` formats files
`validate` checks syntax
`plan` previews changes
`apply` creates infrastructure

🔥 Real Flow of Data Between Modules

VPC module outputs:

vpc_id
public_subnet_ids
private_subnet_ids

These are passed to:

NLB module -> needs VPC and public subnets
EKS module -> needs subnet IDs

Then:

NLB module outputs `nlb_dns`

This is passed to:

Route53 module -> creates DNS record

👉 This is a very important interview point:
Terraform modules communicate using outputs and input variables

🔥 Why This Architecture Is Good

✅ Modular
Each module handles one responsibility

✅ Reusable
You can reuse VPC, EKS, or NLB module in another project

✅ Secure
Worker nodes are in private subnets

✅ Scalable
Multi-AZ subnet structure supports growth

✅ Team-friendly
Remote backend and variables support shared usage

🔥 Current Project Strengths

Uses module-based design
Separates network, DNS, load balancer, and EKS
Uses private subnets for worker nodes
Supports tagging and parameterization
Prepared for remote backend usage

⚠️ Current Improvement Areas

NLB target group is created, but workload attachment still needs to be completed
Single NAT Gateway is okay for learning, but production often uses one NAT per AZ
You can later add CI/CD and environment-specific tfvars files

